The roots given are $2+\sqrt{2}$ and $1-\sqrt{3}$. For the polynomial to have rational coefficients, the conjugates of these roots must also be roots. Therefore, the roots of the polynomial are $2+\sqrt{2}$, $2-\sqrt{2}$, $1-\sqrt{3}$, and $1+\sqrt{3}$. The polynomial can be constructed by forming factors corresponding to these roots and then expanding. The factor corresponding to $2+\sqrt{2}$ is $(x - (2+\sqrt{2}))(x - (2-\sqrt{2})) = (x-2)^2 - (\sqrt{2})^2 = x^2 - 4x + 2$. The factor corresponding to $1-\sqrt{3}$ is $(x - (1-\sqrt{3}))(x - (1+\sqrt{3})) = (x-1)^2 - (\sqrt{3})^2 = x^2 - 2x - 2$. The quartic polynomial is then $(x^2 - 4x + 2)(x^2 - 2x - 2)$. Expanding this, we get $(x^4 - 6x^3 + 8x^2 + 4x - 4)$.
